 water from say  your pool  depending on the various chemicals and  treatments that are used  for your pool or from a hot tub or  something like that  okay but suffice to say if you don't  have at least two weeks of water  i view that as as foolish as not wearing  your seatbelt  on the highway or not having a fire  extinguisher  in your kitchen it's easy insurance it's  inexpensive  it's been proven time and time again to  me through personal experience how  valuable this is  hurricane sandy personal experience  power outages in san francisco  due to all sorts of issues the freeze in  austin texas most recently  which brings me to another suggestion  that is some type of backup  power in my case i have two generators  because i dislike single points of  failure  that can act as backup for full home  power these are fueled by natural gas so  if  needed with some basic maintenance they  could power the entire home  for several weeks that most recently  put my household in a very good position  to help  other people because we had put on our  oxygen mask first  and we had both water and power and  space to spare so this is not only for  yourself if you want to be in a position  to help other people or  those in your community these are basic  precautions okay that's not what i think  the person asking the question intended  me to provide but without safety the  home means nothing  it's all window dressing so number one  is safety needs to feel safe  next there are aesthetic considerations  so if possible i like to have indoor  outdoor  areas and there are ways to  manufacture that if you have large  windows  floor ceiling windows of different types  it's not available to everyone i  understand that but in an ideal world  that would be one component another  would be an open floor plan with  multiple levels  let me explain what i mean by that so i  really like for instance in the kitchen  in particular  a very horizontally expansive feeling  room with steps up it could be even a  single step  and by creating these levels even in say  a 200 square foot space that would be  pretty small  but you get the idea in a kitchen you  could make the kitchen feel twice as  large  by having even a minor level upon which  you then have a separate seating area  for dinner at the  dinner table and this gets into  architecture and  i would encourage people to check out  something called biophilic  design which is a really  interesting rabbit hole to get into bio  right biological philic  leaning towards or liking biophilic  design is worth  exploring green and green  if you want to look at architecture and  design would probably most represent  my aesthetic which is a combination of  let's just call it japanese and northern  californian kind of big sur sln type  architecture so that blend i find and  extremely calming which brings me to the  next point and that is  most items in my house whether it's  artwork  or furniture or otherwise is  selected with the intention to produce a  particular emotional  state and i think about emotional state  a lot because  for most of my life i suffered from  extreme bouts and extended bouts of  depression  so i'm acutely aware of the things that  affect my mood  and inside if you were to look at say my  artwork  and decoration there are a few  consistent points one is there's a lot  of green i have a lot of plants  and i try to ensure that these plants  are  relatively easy to take care of which  isn't that hard to do but  green and plants have an enormous impact  then i have a lot of photography related  to artwork  that includes animals that includes  well humans humans are part of nature  and also scenic shots landscapes  that could be milford sound in new  zealand which i've had a panoramic photo  of for  20 plus years and it's traveled with me  everywhere it could be shots of the  oregon coast  or the rainforests in oregon  photographs of wolves of bears of  jaguars all over the house and then  i have nostalgic pieces that remind me  of one of  the most impactful periods of my life  which was a year in japan as an exchange  student so i have  japanese calligraphy i have a handful of  japanese  artifacts not many but saddles of all  things if you want to see a fun video  search my name and something called  yabusame  that's japanese horseback archery  i'll leave that for you guys to explore  and really that summarizes  the general aesthetic the general  sentiment the general feeling that i  want to cultivate  which is one of safety it's one of being  part of nature not  apart from nature and  uh also a feeling of  not being alone and that just came to  mind because if you walk to my house  you'll notice  almost all of the photographs of humans  have the eyes  looking out so every time you look at  that piece of artwork you feel as though  you're face to face  and connecting with another human being  so those are a few  of the ways in which i make a house feel  like  a home for me  you
 

